Let $\Omega,\Omega'\subset\mathbb R^3$ be bounded domains, let $1\le p<\infty$, and let $H:\Omega\to\Omega'$ be a locally bi-Lipschitz homeomorphism in $W^{1,p}(\Omega;\mathbb R^3)$. For every $\varepsilon>0$ and every continuous function $\xi:\Omega\to(0,\infty)$, there is a $C^\infty$ diffeomorphism $g:\Omega\to\Omega'$ such that $$\label{sm:smoothing-conclusion}
 \left\lVert g-H\right\rVert_{W^{1,p}(\Omega)}<\varepsilon,
 \qquad |g(x)-H(x)|<\xi(x)\quad(x\in\Omega).$$ In particular, $g$ belongs to $W^{1,p}(\Omega;\mathbb R^3)$, and the approximations have exactly the target $\Omega'$.

Let $h:\Omega\to\Omega'$ be an orientation-preserving homeomorphism between bounded domains, and let $g:\Omega\to\mathbb R^3$ be a smooth map with $\det Dg>0$. If $$\label{sm:target-distance}
 |g(x)-h(x)|<\frac12\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(h(x),\mathbb R^3\setminus\Omega')
 \qquad(x\in\Omega),$$ then $g$ is a diffeomorphism of $\Omega$ onto $\Omega'$.
